  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·01r</p>
    <h1>Common Raven Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) has one of the widest ranges of any land bird — resident across the entire Northern Hemisphere from the High Arctic to the Sahara, from Ireland to the Pacific coast of Asia. It occupies diverse habitats but prefers open and semi-open terrain with cliff or tall-tree nest sites. It is absent from densely forested lowland tropics and most of southern Asia.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Global Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven's range spans much of the Northern Hemisphere:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>North America:</strong> resident from northern Alaska and the Canadian Arctic (including Greenland) south through the boreal forest to the northern US. Breeding populations extend south in the Rocky Mountains, Sierra Nevada, Appalachians, and Pacific Coast ranges. Absent from the mid-continental agricultural belt (central Great Plains, Midwest) where the Chihuahuan Raven takes its place in drier areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Europe:</strong> resident across the British Isles (now recovering in England), Scandinavia, Iberian Peninsula, Alps, Carpathians, and Balkans. Absent from lowland northern Europe (Netherlands, northern Germany, Denmark) except as a rare vagrant or recolonist.</li>
      <li><strong>Asia:</strong> resident across Russia (the entire boreal zone from the Urals to the Pacific), the Caucasus, Turkey, the Middle East, Iran, the Himalayan range, Central Asia, and extending into China (Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, Xinjiang). Absent from lowland tropical Asia.</li>
      <li><strong>North Africa:</strong> resident in the Atlas Mountains of Morocco, Algeria, and Tunisia. Absent from the Sahara lowlands (where the Brown-necked Raven replaces it).</li>
      <li><strong>Islands:</strong> resident in the Canary Islands (Lanzarote and Fuerteventura; subspecies <em>C. c. tingitanus</em>), Faroe Islands, and Iceland.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat Preferences</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens occupy a diverse range of habitats:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Optimal:</strong> coastal cliffs, rocky mountains and plateaus, Arctic tundra, boreal forest edges, desert with rocky outcrops. These habitats provide cliff or tall-tree nest sites and open foraging terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Suboptimal but used:</strong> farmland with scattered trees or pylons; urban edges near landfills or slaughterhouses; coniferous plantations (nesting in tall trees).</li>
      <li><strong>Avoided:</strong> closed-canopy lowland forest (limited soaring opportunities); intensively managed agricultural monocultures without structural features.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ravens are remarkably cold-tolerant and are among the few birds that remain in the High Arctic year-round, exploiting marine mammal carcasses, human camps, and seabird colonies for food through the polar winter.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Population Estimates</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Global population estimates are approximate due to the species' vast range:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Global population: estimated at several million individuals — exact figures are uncertain due to inaccessible range in boreal Asia and the Arctic.</li>
      <li>Europe: approximately 300,000–600,000 breeding pairs (BirdLife International).</li>
      <li>Britain: approximately 7,000–8,000 breeding pairs (recovering from historical persecution).</li>
      <li>IUCN status: Least Concern — not globally threatened; stable or increasing in most of its range.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — species hub</a></li>
